## 24. Dutta–Jindal–Pandey–Sinhababu: truncation is already close prior art

**Source:** Pranjal Dutta, Gorav Jindal, Anurag Pandey, Amit Sinhababu, *Arithmetic Circuit Complexity of Division and Truncation*. CCC 2021, Article 25. [Primary proceedings PDF](https://drops.dagstuhl.de/storage/00lipics/lipics-vol200-ccc2021/LIPIcs.CCC.2021.25/LIPIcs.CCC.2021.25.pdf).

- **Problem/input:** Compressed rational/algebraic series; circuit size of their degree-\(d\) truncations.
- **Statements:** **Theorem 6 / 26:** if \(\deg g\le d\), \(\deg h=d_h\), \(g\) has circuit size \(s\), and \(g/h\in\mathbb F[[X]]\), its truncation has circuit size \(\mathrm{poly}(s,d_h,\log d)\).
- **Conditional hardness:** **Theorem 30:** polylogarithmic constant-free circuits for both indicated adjacent truncations of \((1+X)^{-d-1}\) imply \(\tau(t!)=O(\log^{c+1}t)\). **Theorem 32:** small truncations of all \((1+k^2X)^{i/k}\), \(1\le i<k\), imply nonuniform polynomial-time factoring.
- **Hypotheses:** Field setting; constant-free restrictions matter. The low-denominator-degree proof uses factorization over an algebraic closure and derivative identities.
- **Complexity variables:** Denominator degree \(d_h\), not merely its circuit size; distinction between circuit existence and uniform construction.
- **Primitive/relevance:** Particularly close to the proposed compact-prefix operation. No composite-ring extension or efficient general truncation construction is established. **VERIFIED.**

## 25. Power of a sparse polynomial: a precise hardness reduction

**Published attribution:** A matching primary theorem specifically phrased this way was **UNVERIFIED** in this search. The following is a direct reduction, not an attributed novelty claim.

- **Problem/input:** Sparse \(f\in\mathbb Z[X]\), positive integer \(m\), binary target exponent \(K\); decide whether \([X^K]f^m=0\).
- **Derived statement:** Nonzeroness is NP-hard even for unit coefficients.
- **Proof/interface:** For Exact-3-Cover on \(3m\) elements, set \(B=m+1\) and
  \[
  f(X)=\sum_{S}X^{\sum_{j\in S}B^j},
  \qquad K=\sum_{j=1}^{3m}B^j.
  \]
  Choosing \(m\) terms creates no base-\(B\) carries. Thus \([X^K]f^m\) is \(m!\) times the number of exact covers.
- **Representation/cost:** Sparse input and all exponents have polynomial binary length; the power has a compact repeated-squaring circuit.
- **Hypotheses:** Exact characteristic-zero nonnegative coefficients.
- **Composite-ring qualification:** Modulo \(N\), a positive count can disappear, including through \(m!\). Therefore this reduction does not automatically prove the corresponding composite-modulus hardness.
- **Relevance:** Short powering descriptions do not make specified-coefficient extraction generically easy.

## 26. First surviving binomial index: explicit prior formulation

**Source:** OEIS A020639, *Least prime factor of n*; sequence author David W. Wilson; relevant contribution and proof by Stanislav Sykora, 29 July 2017. Current entry consulted. [Primary attributed contribution](https://oeis.org/A020639).

- **Problem/input:** Integer \(N>1\); characterize its smallest prime divisor.
- **Statement:** The contribution identifies it as the smallest \(k\), \(1<k\le N\), for which \(N\nmid\binom Nk\).
- **Proof content:** It explicitly uses
  \[
  k\binom Nk=N\binom{N-1}{k-1}
  \]
  for coprime indices, and a prime-adic valuation argument at a prime divisor.
- **Hypotheses:** All integers \(N>1\), including prime powers. For prime \(N\), the permitted endpoint \(k=N\) survives.
- **Complexity:** A characterization, not a fast support-search algorithm.
- **Primitive/relevance:** Direct prior formulation of the first-index identity and the coprime-index obstruction.
- **Status:** **VERIFIED.** A prior source for the stronger exact residue \(\binom Np\equiv N/p\bmod N\) was **UNVERIFIED**; its proof in the brief remains valid.

## 27. Wilson-factorial folklore: correct the endpoint claim

**Claim audited:** “Computing \((N-1)!\bmod N\) is as hard as factoring.”

- **Status:** False for this exact input/output task; historical attribution of that phrasing is **UNVERIFIED**.
- **Elementary statement:**
  \[
  (N-1)!\equiv
  \begin{cases}
  -1\pmod N,&N\text{ prime},\\
  2\pmod4,&N=4,\\
  0\pmod N,&N>4\text{ composite}.
  \end{cases}
  \]
- **Proof for composites:** Write \(N=ab\), \(1<a\le b<N\). If \(a<b\), both factors occur in the factorial. If \(a=b\ge3\), the distinct terms \(a,2a<N\) have product \(2N\).
- **Input/complexity:** Binary \(N\); the residue follows from polynomial-time primality testing and the \(N=4\) exception. [Primary primality theorem](https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/research/wp-content/uploads/2016/02/primality_journal.pdf).
- **Relevant stronger interface:** Arbitrary binary cutoff \(K\mapsto K!\bmod N\), or just its unit status. Lipton’s reduction and BGS’s bounded-divisor search address that interface.
- **Relevance:** The endpoint factorial loses the information needed for the prefix search.

