The key distinction is rejection versus factor extraction: a failed congruence can fail modulo both hidden primes, leaving its gcd with \(N\) equal to 1. I’m deriving the exact Miller–Rabin distribution, since a sequence’s ability to expose a factor also depends on how much of the odd parts of \(p-1\) and \(q-1\) the exponent kills.

